{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CBDQHPo7xWy4pmwBnF7xGt","type":"tool_result","content":"| 03 forest regime, brute force | 1 | 3 h | 20 | ≤ 6 (C program, ≤ 400 vertices) |\n| 04 SCOOP | 1 | 1.5 h | 15 | 0 |\n| 05 `β⁺` by simulation | 1 | 3 h | 20 | ≤ 8 (the rule at `n ≤ 5000`) |\n| 06 forest regime, on paper | 1 | 3 h | 25 | 0.5 (the brute-force check) |\n| 07 `β⁺` on paper | 1 | 3 h | 25 | 0.2 (recursions) |\n| 08 structured count | 1 | 3 h | 25 | 1 (the `ℓ = 2` floor, the second moment) |\n| 09 ridge hypothesis | 1 | 2 h | 20 | 2 (lattice counts at `n = 20`) |\n| 10 validity | 1 | 2.5 h | 20 | 0.1 |\n| 11 Lean | 1–2 | 4 h | 30 | 0 (lake build) |\n| 12 write-up | 1 | 3 h | 25 | 0 |\n| 13 reserve | 1 | 3 h | 20 | ≤ 4 |\n| **total** | **13–14** | **≈ 35 h** | **≈ $280** | **≤ 25** |\n\nPer-session cap 4 h (`knobs.json`), fix cap 45 min, three fix attempts; the\ngate (lake build, two pytest suites, the paper's `make check`) takes about\n10 min and is run once per item.\n\n## 3. Risks\n\n1. **The forest theorem may be the weak one.** F1 is a factor `ω` loose on a\n   chain of cliques and probably on most forests; if item 03 finds that the\n   true pathwidth of clique trees is additive in `ω` and the depth, the\n   proof of that form is a new argument (the path and centroid lemmas of\n   `TreeBound.lean` do not lift to blocks for free), and one session may\n   not hold it. The plan then ships F1 and the gap.\n2. **`β⁺` from provable constructions may be uninformative.** BFS layers on\n   a graph of mean degree 8 reach width about 0.9 `n`; `n − α` is better but\n   still far from the truth at `e ≳ 3`. The rule is near-exact and\n   unanalysable in a session. The loop may end with `β*` from below, the\n   rule from above by measurement, and no provable constant within 0.3 of\n   either; item 07's kill records exactly that.\n3. **The ridge hypothesis is a coincidence test, not a mechanism.** Twenty-five\n   series at `n = 50–75` with peaks interpolated from three-cell grids can\n   agree by accident at the 0.1-decade level; and §36's real finding — the\n   reachable count — is a first-passage problem over `2^n` states that no\n   item here attempts. A surviving H1 is evidence, not a theory of the\n   ridge.\n\nTwo smaller ones: the machine is shared and loaded (load 27–40 on 32 cores\non the day of writing), so the 4 h caps may bind on items 03 and 05 — both\nare told to sample and say so; and the paper's Appendix E already states\nthe ridge sentence this loop corrects, so item 12 must change a published\ndraft, with the reason recorded.\n\n## 4. What would count as success\n\nA map of every held instance in one parameter with the three lines on it;\nthe one-parameter question answered either way with error bars; a forest\ntheorem proved on paper and in Lean with its tightness measured; `β⁺`\ntabulated by construction with its provable part separated from its\nmeasured part; the structured count tried against a stated threshold; one\nridge hypothesis tested to a kill; the generator's validity stated to the\nexact extent it is proved; and 24 industrial instances described in the\nsame coordinates, with a statement a planner could read, labelled as 24\ninstances. The plan does not promise the constant, the ridge mechanism, or\na theorem above `e_g(r)`.\n----\n\"\"\"Candidate generators for the Chu & Stuckey Random-n-m-d classes.\nMatrix convention here: M is (m patterns) x (n customers), bool.  d = nominal mean customers per pattern.\nEvery candidate returns an accepted matrix or None if the attempt cap is hit; `attempts` is recorded\nthrough the returned tuple (M, tries).\"\"\"\nimport numpy as np\n\ndef mosp_connected(M):\n    m, n = M.shape\n    seen_c = np.zeros(n, bool); seen_c[0] = True\n    while True:\n        seen_p = M[:, seen_c].any(axis=1)\n        new_c = M[seen_p].any(axis=0) | seen_c\n        if new_c.sum() == seen_c.sum():\n            return bool(seen_c.all())\n        seen_c = new_c\n\ndef bern(n, m, p, rng):\n    return rng.random((m, n)) < p\n\ndef fixed(n, m, d, rng):\n    idx = rng.random((m, n)).argsort(axis=1)[:, :d]\n    M = np.zeros((m, n), bool)\n    M[np.arange(m)[:, None], idx] = True\n    return M\n\ndef pad_customers(M, kmin, rng):\n    \"\"\"every customer (column) with fewer than kmin patterns joins random patterns it is not in.\"\"\"\n    m, n = M.shape\n    for j in np.flatnonzero(M.sum(axis=0) < kmin):\n        need = kmin - M[:, j].sum()\n        cand = np.flatnonzero(~M[:, j])\n        M[rng.choice(cand, need, replace=False), j] = True\n    return M\n\ndef pad_patterns(M, kmin, rng):\n    \"\"\"every pattern (row) with fewer than kmin customers gains random customers it does not have.\"\"\"\n    m, n = M.shape\n    for i in np.flatnonzero(M.sum(axis=1) < kmin):\n        need = kmin - M[i].sum()\n        cand = np.flatnonzero(~M[i])\n        M[i, rng.choice(cand, need, replace=False)] = True\n    return M\n\ndef resample_patterns(M, kmin, p, rng):\n    \"\"\"rows with fewer than kmin ones are redrawn (truncated binomial rows).\"\"\"\n    m, n = M.shape\n    while True:\n        bad = np.flatnonzero(M.sum(axis=1) < kmin)\n        if len(bad) == 0: return M\n        M[bad] = rng.random((len(bad), n)) < p\n\ndef with_rejection(draw, cap):\n    def g(n, m, d, rng):\n        for t in range(1, cap + 1):\n            M = draw(n, m, d, rng)\n            if mosp_connected(M): return M, t\n        return None, cap\n    return g\n\ndef no_rejection(draw):\n    def g(n, m, d, rng):\n        return draw(n, m, d, rng), 1\n    return g\n\nCAP = 2000\nCANDIDATES = {\n    # (i) exactly d per pattern, customers padded to >= 2\n    \"i_fixed_padC2\":        no_rejection(lambda n, m, d, r: pad_customers(fixed(n, m, d, r), 2, r)),\n    # (ii) Bernoulli(d/n), customers padded to >= 2\n    \"ii_bern_padC2\":        no_rejection(lambda n, m, d, r: pad_customers(bern(n, m, d / n, r), 2, r)),\n    # (iii) = (i) + discard decomposable\n    \"iii_fixed_padC2_conn\": with_rejection(lambda n, m, d, r: pad_customers(fixed(n, m, d, r), 2, r), CAP),\n    # the paper as written: Bernoulli(d/n), discard decomposable, nothing else\n    \"paper_bern_conn\":      with_rejection(lambda n, m, d, r: bern(n, m, d / n, r), CAP),\n    # Bernoulli, patterns padded to >= 2, discard decomposable\n    \"bern_padP2_conn\":      with_rejection(lambda n, m, d, r: pad_patterns(bern(n, m, d / n, r), 2, r), CAP),\n    # Bernoulli, patterns redrawn until >= 2 (truncated rows), discard decomposable\n    \"bern_truncP2_conn\":    with_rejection(lambda n, m, d, r: resample_patterns(bern(n, m, d / n, r), 2, d / n, r), CAP),\n    # Bernoulli, customers padded to >= 1 then patterns padded to >= 2, discard decomposable\n    \"bern_padC1_padP2_conn\": with_rejection(lambda n, m, d, r: pad_patterns(pad_customers(bern(n, m, d / n, r), 1, r), 2, r), CAP),\n    # same, patterns first then customers\n    \"bern_padP2_padC1_conn\": with_rejection(lambda n, m, d, r: pad_customers(pad_patterns(bern(n, m, d / n, r), 2, r), 1, r), CAP),\n    # Bernoulli, customers padded to >= 1 only, discard decomposable (control: should fail the row minimum)\n    \"bern_padC1_conn\":      with_rejection(lambda n, m, d, r: pad_customers(bern(n, m, d / n, r), 1, r), CAP),\n    # Bernoulli, customers padded to >= 1, patterns to >= 2, no rejection (how much does the discard do?)\n    \"bern_padC1_padP2\":     no_rejection(lambda n, m, d, r: pad_patterns(pad_customers(bern(n, m, d / n, r), 1, r), 2, r)),\n    # fixed d, patterns already >= 2, customers padded to >= 1, discard decomposable\n    \"fixed_padC1_conn\":     with_rejection(lambda n, m, d, r: pad_customers(fixed(n, m, d, r), 1, r), CAP),\n}\n\ndef stats(M):\n    \"\"\"per-instance statistics used for the fit.\"\"\"\n    rs = M.sum(axis=1); cs = M.sum(axis=0)\n    return dict(ones=int(rs.sum()), row_mean=rs.mean(), row_var=rs.var(), row_min=int(rs.min()), row_max=int(rs.max()),\n                col_mean=cs.mean(), col_var=cs.var(), col_min=int(cs.min()), col_max=int(cs.max()),\n                frac_row2=(rs == 2).mean(), frac_row3=(rs == 3).mean(), frac_col1=(cs == 1).mean(), frac_col2=(cs == 2).mean(),\n                rowcol_corr=_rowcol_corr(M, rs, cs))\n\ndef _rowcol_corr(M, rs, cs):\n    # correlation over incidences between the pattern's size and the customer's count\n    i, j = np.nonzero(M)\n    a = rs[i].astype(float); b = cs[j].astype(float)\n    if a.std() == 0 or b.std() == 0: return 0.0\n    return float(np.corrcoef(a, b)[0, 1])\n\n# --- intermediate variants (added after the first fit: padding undershoots the ones count, truncation overshoots) ---\ndef resample_empty_rows(M, p, rng):\n    \"\"\"rows with no ones are redrawn until non-empty; rows with one 1 are left for padding.\"\"\"\n    m, n = M.shape\n    while True:\n        bad = np.flatnonzero(M.sum(axis=1) == 0)\n        if len(bad) == 0: return M\n        M[bad] = rng.random((len(bad), n)) < p\nCANDIDATES.update({\n    # empty patterns redrawn, size-1 patterns padded to 2, empty customers padded to 1, discard decomposable\n    \"bern_redraw0_padP2_padC1_conn\": with_rejection(lambda n, m, d, r: pad_customers(pad_patterns(resample_empty_rows(bern(n, m, d / n, r), d / n, r), 2, r), 1, r), CAP),\n    # the best-fit family with the density lifted 5% (diagnostic: is the residual a density offset?)\n    \"bern105_padC1_padP2_conn\": with_rejection(lambda n, m, d, r: pad_patterns(pad_customers(bern(n, m, 1.05 * d / n, r), 1, r), 2, r), CAP),\n    \"bern110_padC1_padP2_conn\": with_rejection(lambda n, m, d, r: pad_patterns(pad_customers(bern(n, m, 1.10 * d / n, r), 1, r), 2, r), CAP),\n})\n----\n\"\"\"Random MOSP instance generator.\n\nGenerates random binary matrices following the approach from the literature\n(Chu & Stuckey 2009, Faggioli & Bentivoglio 1998).\n\nParameters:\n  - n_patterns: number of products/patterns (columns)\n  - n_customers: number of customers/orders (rows)\n  - density: probability that a customer requires a pattern, or\n  - customers_per_pattern: average number of customers per pattern\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nimport numpy as np\n\nfrom mosp.instance import MOSPInstance\n\n\ndef generate_random_instance(\n    n_customers: int,\n    n_patterns: int,\n    density: float = 0.3,\n    seed: int | None = None,\n    name: str | None = None,\n) -> MOSPInstance:\n    \"\"\"Generate a random MOSP instance with given density.\n\n    Each entry M[i][j] is 1 with probability `density`, independently.\n    Rows/columns that are all-zero are given at least one random 1 to ensure\n    every customer needs something and every pattern is needed by someone.\n\n    Args:\n        n_customers: Number of customers (rows).\n        n_patterns: Number of patterns (columns).\n        density: Probability each entry is 1.\n        seed: Random seed for reproducibility.\n        name: Instance name.\n\n    Returns:\n        A MOSPInstance with the generated binary matrix.\n    \"\"\"\n    rng = np.random.RandomState(seed)\n\n    if name is None:\n        name = f\"random_{n_customers}x{n_patterns}_d{density:.2f}_s{seed}\"\n\n    matrix = (rng.random((n_customers, n_patterns)) < density).astype(np.int8)\n\n    # Ensure no all-zero rows (every customer needs at least one pattern)\n    for i in range(n_customers):\n        if matrix[i].sum() == 0:\n            j = rng.randint(0, n_patterns)\n            matrix[i, j] = 1\n\n    # Ensure no all-zero columns (every pattern is needed by at least one customer)\n    for j in range(n_patterns):\n        if matrix[:, j].sum() == 0:\n            i = rng.randint(0, n_customers)\n            matrix[i, j] = 1\n\n    return MOSPInstance.from_matrix(matrix, name=name)\n\n\ndef generate_structured_instance(\n    n_customers: int,\n    n_patterns: int,\n    customers_per_pattern: int,\n    seed: int | None = None,\n    name: str | None = None,\n) -> MOSPInstance:\n    \"\"\"Generate a MOSP instance where each pattern has a fixed number of customers.\n\n    This follows the generation style from Chu & Stuckey (2009): for each\n    pattern, exactly `customers_per_pattern` customers are randomly selected\n    to require it.\n\n    Args:\n        n_customers: Number of customers (rows).\n        n_patterns: Number of patterns (columns).\n        customers_per_pattern: Exact number of customers requiring each pattern.\n        seed: Random seed for reproducibility.\n        name: Instance name.\n\n    Returns:\n        A MOSPInstance with the generated binary matrix.\n    \"\"\"\n    rng = np.random.RandomState(seed)\n\n    if name is None:\n        name = f\"struct_{n_customers}x{n_patterns}_cpp{customers_per_pattern}_s{seed}\"\n\n    cpp = min(customers_per_pattern, n_customers)\n    matrix = np.zeros((n_customers, n_patterns), dtype=np.int8)\n\n    for j in range(n_patterns):\n        customers = rng.choice(n_customers, size=cpp, replace=False)\n        matrix[customers, j] = 1\n\n    # Ensure no all-zero rows\n    for i in range(n_customers):\n        if matrix[i].sum() == 0:\n            j = rng.randint(0, n_patterns)\n            matrix[i, j] = 1\n\n    return MOSPInstance.from_matrix(matrix, name=name)\n\n\ndef generate_benchmark_suite(\n    output_dir: str | Path,\n    sizes: list[tuple[int, int]] | None = None,\n    densities: list[float] | None = None,\n    n_instances_per_config: int = 3,\n    seed: int = 42,\n) -> list[Path]:\n    \"\"\"Generate a suite of benchmark instances.\n\n    Args:\n        output_dir: Directory to write instance files.\n        sizes: List of (n_customers, n_patterns) pairs.\n        densities: List of density values.\n        n_instances_per_config: Number of instances per (size, density) config.\n        seed: Base random seed.\n\n    Returns:\n        List of paths to generated instance files.\n    \"\"\"\n    output_dir = Path(output_dir)\n    output_dir.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n\n    if sizes is None:\n        sizes = [\n            (10, 10), (15, 10), (10, 15),\n            (15, 15), (20, 15), (20, 20),\n        ]\n    if densities is None:\n        densities = [0.2, 0.3, 0.5]\n\n    paths = []\n    idx = 0\n    for n_cust, n_pat in sizes:\n        for dens in densities:\n            for rep in range(n_instances_per_config):\n                instance = generate_random_instance(\n                    n_customers=n_cust,\n                    n_patterns=n_pat,\n                    density=dens,\n                    seed=seed + idx,\n                    name=f\"bench_{n_cust}x{n_pat}_d{dens:.1f}_r{rep}\",\n                )\n                path = output_dir / f\"{instance.name}.mosp\"\n                instance.to_file(path)\n                paths.append(path)\n                idx += 1\n\n    return paths","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ba2660ed-54b6-4b2f-a3ea-6cf6617bc43b","uuid":"2ba242f1-67cb-47cb-ab9b-fb00cff5d270","timestamp":"2026-11-01T02:25:11.365Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"| 03 forest regime, brute force | 1 | 3 h | 20 | ≤ 6 (C program, ≤ 400 vertices) |\n| 04 SCOOP | 1 | 1.5 h | 15 | 0 |\n| 05 `β⁺` by simulation | 1 | 3 h | 20 | ≤ 8 (the rule at `n ≤ 5000`) |\n| 06 forest regime, on paper | 1 | 3 h | 25 | 0.5 (the brute-force check) |\n| 07 `β⁺` on paper | 1 | 3 h | 25 | 0.2 (recursions) |\n| 08 structured count | 1 | 3 h | 25 | 1 (the `ℓ = 2` floor, the second moment) |\n| 09 ridge hypothesis | 1 | 2 h | 20 | 2 (lattice counts at `n = 20`) |\n| 10 validity | 1 | 2.5 h | 20 | 0.1 |\n| 11 Lean | 1–2 | 4 h | 30 | 0 (lake build) |\n| 12 write-up | 1 | 3 h | 25 | 0 |\n| 13 reserve | 1 | 3 h | 20 | ≤ 4 |\n| **total** | **13–14** | **≈ 35 h** | **≈ $280** | **≤ 25** |\n\nPer-session cap 4 h (`knobs.json`), fix cap 45 min, three fix attempts; the\ngate (lake build, two pytest suites, the paper's `make check`) takes about\n10 min and is run once per item.\n\n## 3. Risks\n\n1. **The forest theorem may be the weak one.** F1 is a factor `ω` loose on a\n   chain of cliques and probably on most forests; if item 03 finds that the\n   true pathwidth of clique trees is additive in `ω` and the depth, the\n   proof of that form is a new argument (the path and centroid lemmas of\n   `TreeBound.lean` do not lift to blocks for free), and one session may\n   not hold it. The plan then ships F1 and the gap.\n2. **`β⁺` from provable constructions may be uninformative.** BFS layers on\n   a graph of mean degree 8 reach width about 0.9 `n`; `n − α` is better but\n   still far from the truth at `e ≳ 3`. The rule is near-exact and\n   unanalysable in a session. The loop may end with `β*` from below, the\n   rule from above by measurement, and no provable constant within 0.3 of\n   either; item 07's kill records exactly that.\n3. **The ridge hypothesis is a coincidence test, not a mechanism.** Twenty-five\n   series at `n = 50–75` with peaks interpolated from three-cell grids can\n   agree by accident at the 0.1-decade level; and §36's real finding — the\n   reachable count — is a first-passage problem over `2^n` states that no\n   item here attempts. A surviving H1 is evidence, not a theory of the\n   ridge.\n\nTwo smaller ones: the machine is shared and loaded (load 27–40 on 32 cores\non the day of writing), so the 4 h caps may bind on items 03 and 05 — both\nare told to sample and say so; and the paper's Appendix E already states\nthe ridge sentence this loop corrects, so item 12 must change a published\ndraft, with the reason recorded.\n\n## 4. What would count as success\n\nA map of every held instance in one parameter with the three lines on it;\nthe one-parameter question answered either way with error bars; a forest\ntheorem proved on paper and in Lean with its tightness measured; `β⁺`\ntabulated by construction with its provable part separated from its\nmeasured part; the structured count tried against a stated threshold; one\nridge hypothesis tested to a kill; the generator's validity stated to the\nexact extent it is proved; and 24 industrial instances described in the\nsame coordinates, with a statement a planner could read, labelled as 24\ninstances. The plan does not promise the constant, the ridge mechanism, or\na theorem above `e_g(r)`.\n----\n\"\"\"Candidate generators for the Chu & Stuckey Random-n-m-d classes.\nMatrix convention here: M is (m patterns) x (n customers), bool.  d = nominal mean customers per pattern.\nEvery candidate returns an accepted matrix or None if the attempt cap is hit; `attempts` is recorded\nthrough the returned tuple (M, tries).\"\"\"\nimport numpy as np\n\ndef mosp_connected(M):\n    m, n = M.shape\n    seen_c = np.zeros(n, bool); seen_c[0] = True\n    while True:\n        seen_p = M[:, seen_c].any(axis=1)\n        new_c = M[seen_p].any(axis=0) | seen_c\n        if new_c.sum() == seen_c.sum():\n            return bool(seen_c.all())\n        seen_c = new_c\n\ndef bern(n, m, p, rng):\n    return rng.random((m, n)) < p\n\ndef fixed(n, m, d, rng):\n    idx = rng.random((m, n)).argsort(axis=1)[:, :d]\n    M = np.zeros((m, n), bool)\n    M[np.arange(m)[:, None], idx] = True\n    return M\n\ndef pad_customers(M, kmin, rng):\n    \"\"\"every customer (column) with fewer than kmin patterns joins random patterns it is not in.\"\"\"\n    m, n = M.shape\n    for j in np.flatnonzero(M.sum(axis=0) < kmin):\n        need = kmin - M[:, j].sum()\n        cand = np.flatnonzero(~M[:, j])\n        M[rng.choice(cand, need, replace=False), j] = True\n    return M\n\ndef pad_patterns(M, kmin, rng):\n    \"\"\"every pattern (row) with fewer than kmin customers gains random customers it does not have.\"\"\"\n    m, n = M.shape\n    for i in np.flatnonzero(M.sum(axis=1) < kmin):\n        need = kmin - M[i].sum()\n        cand = np.flatnonzero(~M[i])\n        M[i, rng.choice(cand, need, replace=False)] = True\n    return M\n\ndef resample_patterns(M, kmin, p, rng):\n    \"\"\"rows with fewer than kmin ones are redrawn (truncated binomial rows).\"\"\"\n    m, n = M.shape\n    while True:\n        bad = np.flatnonzero(M.sum(axis=1) < kmin)\n        if len(bad) == 0: return M\n        M[bad] = rng.random((len(bad), n)) < p\n\ndef with_rejection(draw, cap):\n    def g(n, m, d, rng):\n        for t in range(1, cap + 1):\n            M = draw(n, m, d, rng)\n            if mosp_connected(M): return M, t\n        return None, cap\n    return g\n\ndef no_rejection(draw):\n    def g(n, m, d, rng):\n        return draw(n, m, d, rng), 1\n    return g\n\nCAP = 2000\nCANDIDATES = {\n    # (i) exactly d per pattern, customers padded to >= 2\n    \"i_fixed_padC2\":        no_rejection(lambda n, m, d, r: pad_customers(fixed(n, m, d, r), 2, r)),\n    # (ii) Bernoulli(d/n), customers padded to >= 2\n    \"ii_bern_padC2\":        no_rejection(lambda n, m, d, r: pad_customers(bern(n, m, d / n, r), 2, r)),\n    # (iii) = (i) + discard decomposable\n    \"iii_fixed_padC2_conn\": with_rejection(lambda n, m, d, r: pad_customers(fixed(n, m, d, r), 2, r), CAP),\n    # the paper as written: Bernoulli(d/n), discard decomposable, nothing else\n    \"paper_bern_conn\":      with_rejection(lambda n, m, d, r: bern(n, m, d / n, r), CAP),\n    # Bernoulli, patterns padded to >= 2, discard decomposable\n    \"bern_padP2_conn\":      with_rejection(lambda n, m, d, r: pad_patterns(bern(n, m, d / n, r), 2, r), CAP),\n    # Bernoulli, patterns redrawn until >= 2 (truncated rows), discard decomposable\n    \"bern_truncP2_conn\":    with_rejection(lambda n, m, d, r: resample_patterns(bern(n, m, d / n, r), 2, d / n, r), CAP),\n    # Bernoulli, customers padded to >= 1 then patterns padded to >= 2, discard decomposable\n    \"bern_padC1_padP2_conn\": with_rejection(lambda n, m, d, r: pad_patterns(pad_customers(bern(n, m, d / n, r), 1, r), 2, r), CAP),\n    # same, patterns first then customers\n    \"bern_padP2_padC1_conn\": with_rejection(lambda n, m, d, r: pad_customers(pad_patterns(bern(n, m, d / n, r), 2, r), 1, r), CAP),\n    # Bernoulli, customers padded to >= 1 only, discard decomposable (control: should fail the row minimum)\n    \"bern_padC1_conn\":      with_rejection(lambda n, m, d, r: pad_customers(bern(n, m, d / n, r), 1, r), CAP),\n    # Bernoulli, customers padded to >= 1, patterns to >= 2, no rejection (how much does the discard do?)\n    \"bern_padC1_padP2\":     no_rejection(lambda n, m, d, r: pad_patterns(pad_customers(bern(n, m, d / n, r), 1, r), 2, r)),\n    # fixed d, patterns already >= 2, customers padded to >= 1, discard decomposable\n    \"fixed_padC1_conn\":     with_rejection(lambda n, m, d, r: pad_customers(fixed(n, m, d, r), 1, r), CAP),\n}\n\ndef stats(M):\n    \"\"\"per-instance statistics used for the fit.\"\"\"\n    rs = M.sum(axis=1); cs = M.sum(axis=0)\n    return dict(ones=int(rs.sum()), row_mean=rs.mean(), row_var=rs.var(), row_min=int(rs.min()), row_max=int(rs.max()),\n                col_mean=cs.mean(), col_var=cs.var(), col_min=int(cs.min()), col_max=int(cs.max()),\n                frac_row2=(rs == 2).mean(), frac_row3=(rs == 3).mean(), frac_col1=(cs == 1).mean(), frac_col2=(cs == 2).mean(),\n                rowcol_corr=_rowcol_corr(M, rs, cs))\n\ndef _rowcol_corr(M, rs, cs):\n    # correlation over incidences between the pattern's size and the customer's count\n    i, j = np.nonzero(M)\n    a = rs[i].astype(float); b = cs[j].astype(float)\n    if a.std() == 0 or b.std() == 0: return 0.0\n    return float(np.corrcoef(a, b)[0, 1])\n\n# --- intermediate variants (added after the first fit: padding undershoots the ones count, truncation overshoots) ---\ndef resample_empty_rows(M, p, rng):\n    \"\"\"rows with no ones are redrawn until non-empty; rows with one 1 are left for padding.\"\"\"\n    m, n = M.shape\n    while True:\n        bad = np.flatnonzero(M.sum(axis=1) == 0)\n        if len(bad) == 0: return M\n        M[bad] = rng.random((len(bad), n)) < p\nCANDIDATES.update({\n    # empty patterns redrawn, size-1 patterns padded to 2, empty customers padded to 1, discard decomposable\n    \"bern_redraw0_padP2_padC1_conn\": with_rejection(lambda n, m, d, r: pad_customers(pad_patterns(resample_empty_rows(bern(n, m, d / n, r), d / n, r), 2, r), 1, r), CAP),\n    # the best-fit family with the density lifted 5% (diagnostic: is the residual a density offset?)\n    \"bern105_padC1_padP2_conn\": with_rejection(lambda n, m, d, r: pad_patterns(pad_customers(bern(n, m, 1.05 * d / n, r), 1, r), 2, r), CAP),\n    \"bern110_padC1_padP2_conn\": with_rejection(lambda n, m, d, r: pad_patterns(pad_customers(bern(n, m, 1.10 * d / n, r), 1, r), 2, r), CAP),\n})\n----\n\"\"\"Random MOSP instance generator.\n\nGenerates random binary matrices following the approach from the literature\n(Chu & Stuckey 2009, Faggioli & Bentivoglio 1998).\n\nParameters:\n  - n_patterns: number of products/patterns (columns)\n  - n_customers: number of customers/orders (rows)\n  - density: probability that a customer requires a pattern, or\n  - customers_per_pattern: average number of customers per pattern\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nimport numpy as np\n\nfrom mosp.instance import MOSPInstance\n\n\ndef generate_random_instance(\n    n_customers: int,\n    n_patterns: int,\n    density: float = 0.3,\n    seed: int | None = None,\n    name: str | None = None,\n) -> MOSPInstance:\n    \"\"\"Generate a random MOSP instance with given density.\n\n    Each entry M[i][j] is 1 with probability `density`, independently.\n    Rows/columns that are all-zero are given at least one random 1 to ensure\n    every customer needs something and every pattern is needed by someone.\n\n    Args:\n        n_customers: Number of customers (rows).\n        n_patterns: Number of patterns (columns).\n        density: Probability each entry is 1.\n        seed: Random seed for reproducibility.\n        name: Instance name.\n\n    Returns:\n        A MOSPInstance with the generated binary matrix.\n    \"\"\"\n    rng = np.random.RandomState(seed)\n\n    if name is None:\n        name = f\"random_{n_customers}x{n_patterns}_d{density:.2f}_s{seed}\"\n\n    matrix = (rng.random((n_customers, n_patterns)) < density).astype(np.int8)\n\n    # Ensure no all-zero rows (every customer needs at least one pattern)\n    for i in range(n_customers):\n        if matrix[i].sum() == 0:\n            j = rng.randint(0, n_patterns)\n            matrix[i, j] = 1\n\n    # Ensure no all-zero columns (every pattern is needed by at least one customer)\n    for j in range(n_patterns):\n        if matrix[:, j].sum() == 0:\n            i = rng.randint(0, n_customers)\n            matrix[i, j] = 1\n\n    return MOSPInstance.from_matrix(matrix, name=name)\n\n\ndef generate_structured_instance(\n    n_customers: int,\n    n_patterns: int,\n    customers_per_pattern: int,\n    seed: int | None = None,\n    name: str | None = None,\n) -> MOSPInstance:\n    \"\"\"Generate a MOSP instance where each pattern has a fixed number of customers.\n\n    This follows the generation style from Chu & Stuckey (2009): for each\n    pattern, exactly `customers_per_pattern` customers are randomly selected\n    to require it.\n\n    Args:\n        n_customers: Number of customers (rows).\n        n_patterns: Number of patterns (columns).\n        customers_per_pattern: Exact number of customers requiring each pattern.\n        seed: Random seed for reproducibility.\n        name: Instance name.\n\n    Returns:\n        A MOSPInstance with the generated binary matrix.\n    \"\"\"\n    rng = np.random.RandomState(seed)\n\n    if name is None:\n        name = f\"struct_{n_customers}x{n_patterns}_cpp{customers_per_pattern}_s{seed}\"\n\n    cpp = min(customers_per_pattern, n_customers)\n    matrix = np.zeros((n_customers, n_patterns), dtype=np.int8)\n\n    for j in range(n_patterns):\n        customers = rng.choice(n_customers, size=cpp, replace=False)\n        matrix[customers, j] = 1\n\n    # Ensure no all-zero rows\n    for i in range(n_customers):\n        if matrix[i].sum() == 0:\n            j = rng.randint(0, n_patterns)\n            matrix[i, j] = 1\n\n    return MOSPInstance.from_matrix(matrix, name=name)\n\n\ndef generate_benchmark_suite(\n    output_dir: str | Path,\n    sizes: list[tuple[int, int]] | None = None,\n    densities: list[float] | None = None,\n    n_instances_per_config: int = 3,\n    seed: int = 42,\n) -> list[Path]:\n    \"\"\"Generate a suite of benchmark instances.\n\n    Args:\n        output_dir: Directory to write instance files.\n        sizes: List of (n_customers, n_patterns) pairs.\n        densities: List of density values.\n        n_instances_per_config: Number of instances per (size, density) config.\n        seed: Base random seed.\n\n    Returns:\n        List of paths to generated instance files.\n    \"\"\"\n    output_dir = Path(output_dir)\n    output_dir.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n\n    if sizes is None:\n        sizes = [\n            (10, 10), (15, 10), (10, 15),\n            (15, 15), (20, 15), (20, 20),\n        ]\n    if densities is None:\n        densities = [0.2, 0.3, 0.5]\n\n    paths = []\n    idx = 0\n    for n_cust, n_pat in sizes:\n        for dens in densities:\n            for rep in range(n_instances_per_config):\n                instance = generate_random_instance(\n                    n_customers=n_cust,\n                    n_patterns=n_pat,\n                    density=dens,\n                    seed=seed + idx,\n                    name=f\"bench_{n_cust}x{n_pat}_d{dens:.1f}_r{rep}\",\n                )\n                path = output_dir / f\"{instance.name}.mosp\"\n                instance.to_file(path)\n                paths.append(path)\n                idx += 1\n\n    return paths","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01CBDQHPo7xWy4pmwBnF7xGt","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
