two_key_closing_order(instance: MOSPInstance) -> list[int]:\n499-    \"\"\"The two-key closing rule of `reports/ml_nature.md` §7, followed greedily.\n500-\n501-    At every step, over *every* customer not yet closed (not only the open\n502-    frontier), pick the one that\n503-\n504-    1. opens the fewest new stacks: `|N[c] \\\\ opened|`, `N` self-inclusive;\n505-    2. on ties, has the most unclosed neighbours: `|N[c] \\\\ closed| - 1`;\n506-    3. on ties, has the fewest products not yet produced; then the lowest index.\n507-\n508-    Keys 3 are MCN's own tie-break (`least_cost_node`), so the rule is\n509-    `learning.distil.lex_key(HYPOTHESES[\"lex:min newly_opened,max\n510-    remaining_degree\"])` computed with popcounts instead of a feature matrix;\n511-    `tests/test_heuristics.py` checks the two agree. Key 1 is the cheapest-first\n512-    order in which `restricted_dfs` expands its fan, so the greedy is that\n513-    search's first leaf; key 2 is the *reverse* of MCN's minimum degree.\n514-\n515-    Held out over 1,920 corpus instances the rule's construction is 0.348 stacks\n516-    over the optimum against 1.616 for MCN and 0.519 for the LightGBM ranker\n517-    (`reports/ml_nature.md` §7). Returns only customers with at least one\n518-    product, as `restricted_dfs`'s `seed_order` expects.\n519-    \"\"\"\n520-    masks = _neighbour_masks(instance)\n521-    n = instance.n_customers\n522-    pmasks = [0] * n\n523-    for c in range(n):\n524-        for p in instance.customer_patterns(c):\n525-            pmasks[c] |= 1 << p\n526-    remaining = [c for c in range(n) if masks[c]]\n527-    closed = opened = produced = 0\n528-    order: list[int] = []\n529-    while remaining:\n530-        pick = min(\n531-            remaining,\n532-            key=lambda c: (\n533-                (masks[c] & ~opened).bit_count(),\n534-                -((masks[c] & ~closed).bit_count() - 1),\n535-                (pmasks[c] & ~produced).bit_count(),\n536-                c,